p1 range. That will tell me the stroke assignment.\n\nThen determine the aspect: bar length (p1 range about 300) vs bar separation (p2 range about ?). If separation is about 115, aspect is 2.6.\n\nI think the intended letter is probably \"Z\", because when drawn with x horizontal (the natural reading of the PDB's XY view), it looks like a Z, and the benchmark generator probably drew the letter in the XY plane (x right, y up) and then applied a random 3D rotation. The XY projection showed a clean Z, and XZ also showed a Z-like shape because the rotation kept x roughly horizontal.\n\nBut the N reading requires viewing with x vertical. If the generator drew with x = horizontal width, then a letter with long horizontal bars and short vertical separation is a Z (stretched). If it had drawn an N, the long strokes would be vertical (along y), i.e., the long axis would be the vertical strokes, and the two long parallel bars would be vertical. In our data, the two parallel bars run along the long axis. For N, the parallel bars (vertical strokes) run along the height axis = the long axis (307), separated by the width (115). For Z, the parallel bars run along the width = long axis, separated by the height (115).\n\nUgh, same thing again. The line drawings are identical; only the semantic assignment of width vs height differs, and that's unobservable except through the prior of aspect ratio. A Z stretched to 2.7:1 vs an N squished to 0.37:1. Typical fonts: Z w/h about 0.85, N w/h about 0.75. Our shape would require 3x stretching for Z, or 2x squishing for N.\n\nMaybe the generator used a wide layout? Hmm.\n\nLet me look for more clues: the direction of the diagonal relative to the bars distinguishes Z from N only via orientation (already established). What about the chirality of the 3D arrangement? No — a 3D rotation can't turn Z into N; that requires a 90° in-plane rotation, which is a legitimate 3D rotation about the plane normal. So truly ambiguous as a line drawing; only th