{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"reasoning","text":"The PC1–PC2 view shows a clear zigzag: top bar, diagonal line, bottom bar. PC1–PC3 also shows a zigzag (meaning the zigzag exists in both projections — the character plane is tilted, or the object is a 3D zigzag). PC2–PC3 shows a diagonal band (the character plane is tilted about 40° around the PC1 axis… whatever).\n\nSo the object is a 3-stroke zigzag. Character face dimensions: length 312 × height 134 (PC1–PC2), but PC1–PC3 also shows a zigzag with height 93. The true character plane is probably some combination; the in-plane vertical extent is probably sqrt(134²+93²)? No wait — if the character plane is tilted with respect to the PCA axes… PCA should find the plane of maximum variance; if the character is a planar zigzag, PC1 and PC2 should span the character plane and PC3 should be the thickness. But PC3's range is 93, which is too large for thickness. Unless the character isn't planar: for example, the top bar is at one depth and the bottom bar at another (like a 3D zigzag, e.g. a \"Z\" drawn in 3D with the strokes not coplanar).\n\nActually, if the character plane were tilted with respect to the PCA… no, PCA aligns with the plane of maximum variance; for a planar object, PC3 = thickness (small). Here PC3's range is 93 Å — not small. So the object really is 3D: the zigzag appears in two projections.\n\nInterpretation: maybe the character was drawn in 3D: e.g. top bar along x at (y+, z+), bottom bar along x at (y-, z-), diagonal line connecting them. So the character plane is actually tilted: the vertical axis of the character = a combination of y and z. Then the true in-plane height = sqrt(134² + 93²)?? No: if the character is planar with vertical v = a combination of PC2 and PC3, then PCA would have found v as PC2 (max variance). The variance in the PC2 direction is 134 range, PC3 is 93 range. For a planar character, all points lie in the plane spanned by PC1 and some in-plane direction