 134 → aspect 2.2. As an N: width 134, height 300 → aspect 0.45.\n\nHmm, hmm. Let me think about the number of helices: chains A (903) and D (903) are identical in length; B, E are 143; C, F are 148. Pairs! (A,D), (B,E), (C,F) — suggesting the character is drawn twice (two layers, front and back), i.e. the character outline is duplicated in the depth direction. So the character = 1 layer of chains A, B, C (or D, E, F), and the two layers are separated in the depth direction (about 90 Å?). That explains the PC3 range of 93: two layers about 50–90 apart.\n\nSo one layer = chain A (long, tracing top bar + diagonal + bottom bar? 903 residues ≈ 903*1.5 Å ≈ 1350 Å of contour length; a zigzag of 300+330+300 ≈ 930 Å plus linkers… plausible) plus B (143 residues ≈ 215 Å) and C (148 ≈ 222 Å). Hmm, if A traces the entire zigzag, what are B and C? Maybe A traces only part.\n\nLet me analyze the strokes of layer 1 (chains A, B, C) in the character frame. Classify points into top bar / diagonal / bottom bar by position along the vertical axis, and see which chain covers which stroke. This will give stroke lengths: bar length vs separation. But we already know those.\n\nThe Z vs N decision still comes down to aspect ratio / orientation convention. Let me think about which is more likely from the benchmark designer's perspective: they would generate the character with a natural aspect ratio (roughly square). Our character face is 312 × about 140 (in-plane vertical, combining PC2 and the tilt? Let me compute the true plane: if the two layers are separated along the normal n, the in-plane vertical is… let me compute the plane containing the strokes directly: fit a plane to the stroke centers.\n\nSimpler: compute the centers of the 3 strokes (top bar, diagonal, bottom bar) in 3D, get the 3D vector of the bar direction (≈ PC1) and the 3D vector from the bottom bar center to the top bar center = the vertical v. Then aspect = |bar length| / |v|. The bar length along PC1 is about 300; |v|