hing. They are rotations of each other. So the benchmark's ground truth is whichever the designer picked, and the shape was generated in some orientation; the designer knows the original orientation. We observe the shape in some coordinate frame. If the designer applied a known/random rotation, we can't invert it without knowing the convention.\n\nBut: maybe the designer didn't rotate at all! Maybe the character was drawn directly in the XY plane (x right, y up), with no rotation, and the z variation comes from the 2-layer depth and helix wobble. Check: z range 96 vs y range 115. If drawn in XY with depth in z, the XZ projection should not show a clean zigzag — but it does. Hmm, unless the two layers are separated in z AND the bars are separated in y… wait, the XZ zigzag requires the top and bottom bars to be at different z. If the layers are separated in z (front layer z≈+40, back layer z≈-40) and each layer contains both top and bottom bars, then the XZ projection shows two horizontal bands (at z+ and z-), each spanning the full x range — that would look like an \"=\" sign, not a Z. But we saw a Z with a diagonal in XZ. The diagonal in XZ means z varies continuously with x along the diagonal stroke — that happens if the diagonal connects the front-layer top bar to the back-layer bottom bar??\n\nI'm overthinking. Let me directly examine the 3D stroke structure: cluster the CA points into strokes and output the 3D endpoints of each stroke. Then reconstruct the skeleton and see exactly what this 3D object is.\n\nLet me do a proper skeleton analysis:\n1. Use chains A, B, C (layer 1?) — first verify the layer hypothesis: compare the XY traces of chain A and chain D: if they are nearly identical in XY but offset in z, then layers.\n\nFrom the diag_chains figure: chain A XY and chain D XY looked similar (both Z-like?). Hmm, actually chain A's XY looked like a full zigzag, and D also looked like a full zigzag. And B, C, E, F looked like short bars.\n\nNew idea: forget the