de Z. The designer may have stretched it. No problem.\n\nBut hmm, what if the designer applied a full random rotation? Then PC1≈x is coincidence. The rotation matrix looks like about 35° about the x-axis (PC2 = 0.085, 0.83, -0.552; PC3 = -0.02, -0.55, -0.83). That's a rotation of about 33–56° about x. Small tilt about other axes (0.085, 0.028). So the applied rotation ≈ rotation about the x-axis only. It's very likely the designer's rotation was either about a single axis, or none (the tilt might be part of the design, e.g. to make it 3D). Either way, reading in the XY frame gives Z.\n\nGiven the candidate set and the visual appearance, I'll go with Z with fairly high confidence (0.8), noting the orientation: the character plane is tilted about 35–40° about the long (x) axis; read with x right, y up, it's a Z (top bar at +y, diagonal from top-right to bottom-left, bottom bar at -y).\n\nWait, but let me double-check the diagonal direction in XY: from the image, the diagonal goes from top-right to bottom-left → Z (not an S/mirrored Z). A mirrored Z (Ƹ-shape, i.e. an \"S\" without curves) is not in the candidate list anyway; and a mirrored Z = Z under 180° rotation (Z has 180° rotational symmetry). N also has 180° symmetry. So no mirror issue.\n\nLet me also double-check against other candidates: X would show two crossing diagonals — not present. T: one bar + one vertical — not present. V: two diagonals — not present. L: one vertical + one horizontal — not present. H: two verticals + a middle bar — not present. So among the candidates, only Z/N match; by orientation convention, choose Z.\n\nHmm, but honestly the Z vs N problem: let me consider once more whether the benchmark's oracle character could be N and the shape was drawn with the vertical strokes along x?? That would be a strange drawing convention (character lying on its side). The natural convention is upright in XY. I'll go with Z.\n\nConfidence: the zigzag is unambiguous; the Z vs N orientation ambiguity rem